    {
      "id": "159T8Ch0ToDEzx5KZMPSe",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -699,
      "y": -579.5,
      "width": 798.139404296875,
      "height": 850,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b6o",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 489382528,
      "version": 1671,
      "versionNonce": 1959531904,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789488333720,
      "created": 1789486909308,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "4 main issues with these two \nendpoint\n\n1. state inconsistency ( user might \ntry to do a invalid state change \nexample -> pending to directly completed.)\n\n  my first thought to solve this :-\n        \n            a. first i will retrieve the current status of \nthe job from backend .\n             b. i will create a function called \n        \n\nfunction checkValidState(currentState, requestedSTate) \n- this function will have its state validation logic to identify\nthat is the transition is valid or not ?\n\n\n2. COncurrency issue :- for this particuler case, the \nlocking or priority approach would be overhead, i will\nmake a because rule must be enforeced to the first come based.\nso i actually needs to have a strong state transition validation logic\n\n    and year actually i can put the entire request in a transaction, it will \nensure the atomicity while updating the state.\n\n\n\n3.  bypassing the frontend and giving wrong state names inside the request\npayload\n, it is simple to handle via enums, we can define the 4 state enums, if requested \nstate change belong to them i should allow to further so i can make a\nmiddleware for that.   ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "4 main issues with these two \nendpoint\n\n1. state inconsistency ( user might \ntry to do a invalid state change \nexample -> pending to directly completed.)\n\n  my first thought to solve this :-\n        \n            a. first i will retrieve the current status of \nthe job from backend .\n             b. i will create a function called \n        \n\nfunction checkValidState(currentState, requestedSTate) \n- this function will have its state validation logic to identify\nthat is the transition is valid or not ?\n\n\n2. COncurrency issue :- for this particuler case, the \nlocking or priority approach would be overhead, i will\nmake a because rule must be enforeced to the first come based.\nso i actually needs to have a strong state transition validation logic\n\n    and year actually i can put the entire request in a transaction, it will \nensure the atomicity while updating the state.\n\n\n\n3.  bypassing the frontend and giving wrong state names inside the request payload\n, it is simple to handle via enums, we can define the 4 state enums, if requested \nstate change belong to them i should allow to further so i can make a\nmiddleware for that.   ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
